00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:13.740
BRANDON BAKER: Thank you, Andy.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.010
So how many of you here
remember hearing about Spectre

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:18.070
and Meltdown back in January?

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:18.640
Right.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:22.290
It's a pretty
fundamental discovery,

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:25.260
and what made it so
interesting and so unique

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:28.650
was that side channels
as a vulnerability class

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:29.850
had been known for a while.

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:32.320
There was research in the
early 2000s that said look,

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.070
if you have a crypto algorithm
and it accesses memory

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:38.070
in a certain pattern based
on bits being in a key,

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:40.620
you could use that
to derive information

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.630
about that program's behavior.

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:45.750
What was different with Spectre
and Meltdown was a realization

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:49.710
that most modern
CPUs are constantly

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:51.630
running a speculative engine--

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:53.760
another set of
programs, basically,

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.200
that you don't
have control over,

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.060
which are racing
ahead and trying

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:00.150
to fetch memory that might
be useful to the real program

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:01.800
when it gets around later.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:04.560
And it'll do things like try
to speculate and estimate

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:07.050
which branches might
get taken, and what

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:09.840
interesting memory, then,
is useful to try to pull in.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:11.850
And it's this
speculative behavior

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:14.470
that turns out actually
leaks a lot of information--

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:17.160
a lot more than anyone
really realized.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:19.020
So much so, actually,
that you can read out

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:23.010
the entire contents of memory
from an unprivileged domain,

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:26.550
whether it be user space
process or a virtual machine.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:28.530
And what this had
is the potential

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:31.234
for an impact to
affect how VMs could

00:08:31.234 --> 00:08:32.400
be isolated from each other.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:35.460
Or how a process could be
isolated from its operating

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:36.270
system.

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:39.720
Or how a JavaScript applet could
be isolated from the browser

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:41.850
that it's running.

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:43.860
So with this
discovery in mind, I

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.470
want to take you through a
little bit of an inside look

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.440
of what this looked like at
Google in responding to this,

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:53.070
with a focus around Cloud, but
also a realization that this

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:56.980
affected and impacted
the entire industry.

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:59.280
So we'll start the wayback
machine here, back to June

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:04.020
last summer, when Project
0 discovered this issue.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:07.270
There was a period of time, then
we transition from July time

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:10.560
frame, where with this as a
discovery and a realization,

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.190
how are we going to fix this?

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:14.310
How could we actually
respond to this?

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:17.490
This is a fairly fundamental
flaw in modern CPUs

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:19.320
that we all depend on.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.790
We needed to respond, though,
because the potential impact

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:23.710
to the industry was too great.

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:26.880
But we also had to develop
mitigations and workarounds

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.640
in such a way that didn't
really impact performance.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:31.140
That was a very
important element

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:32.639
to all the consideration
I think you

00:09:32.639 --> 00:09:34.280
were doing in this time frame.

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:36.210
Did the fix have to
come from microcode?

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:37.115
Did it have to
come from hardware?

00:09:37.115 --> 00:09:38.730
Did if have to
come from software?

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:40.770
Were there things we
could do in the compiler?

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:44.370
This is this time frame
where a lot of experts

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:47.250
from a wide variety of
disciplines and fields

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:51.240
came together and spent the
time to think through this.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.910
And I'll talk a little bit more
about what those mitigations

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:55.620
were, and why they
were effective,

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:58.710
and how we got those
out into production.

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:02.100
Because deployment obviously is
a very serious consideration.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:04.200
This was a set of
flaws that we had

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:09.960
to basically patch every
machine at Google to address.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:11.910
And then it moves forward
to this last period

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:14.050
of time frame for disclosure.

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:16.080
Coordinated responsible
disclosure--

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:19.260
super important, given
the wide impact of this

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:21.030
affecting the entire industry.

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:24.010
And I have to say, as we were
going through this time frame,

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:26.550
every day, every week, was
always this concern around,

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:28.340
was this going to be leaked?

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.050
Was somebody going to
also co-discover this?

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:32.970
There was a security
researcher in July

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:36.000
who made a really, really
close discovery of something

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.920
very similar to this
set of problems,

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:40.310
but couldn't quite get the
implementation details working

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:41.730
and quite right.

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:44.100
And so there was
a brief kind of--

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:46.350
OK-- can pause and
get back into this.

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:49.050
Because it was important that
we also made sure that not just

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:51.180
Google, but the
whole industry was

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:54.650
ready to go when it was
time to announce this issue.

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:00.350
And as part of that thinking
through this timeline of--

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:05.020
OK, where are people thinking
about side channel attacks--

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:06.070
speculative execution?

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:08.620
How much interest
is there going on?

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:10.570
Is there awareness
of this issue--

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:12.130
thinking through this trend?

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:16.330
And amazingly, we were
actually able to keep this

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:18.970
as a coordinated disclosure.

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:22.190
And really, it wasn't
until January 3

00:11:22.190 --> 00:11:25.240
when this actually
came to light--

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:28.262
mostly to plan.

00:11:28.262 --> 00:11:30.220
So I'm going to talk
about some of the details,

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:32.740
kind of a refresher or
maybe sort of a deeper dive

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:34.930
into how this thing
actually works

00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:37.450
and what the variants were.

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:40.600
There are many
variants of this class.

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:43.930
Because in some sense, this is
a new class of vulnerabilities.

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:46.960
And at the time, the June
discoveries of last year--

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:49.360
the ones we were dealing
with responding to--

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:51.622
these were the three variants
that we were looking at.

00:11:51.622 --> 00:11:53.080
And I want to call
these out to you

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:55.750
because each one required
a different mitigation

00:11:55.750 --> 00:11:59.270
and a different
part of our stack.

00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:01.140
So the first variant
here is something

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:02.847
we call Bounds Checks Bypass.

00:12:02.847 --> 00:12:04.930
I mentioned there's that
speculative engine that's

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:06.850
always racing ahead.

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:08.680
It skips a lot of
important checks--

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:10.870
things that normal software,
when it actually really

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:13.180
does finally execute, will do.

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:15.970
For example, check
within the bounds arrays,

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:19.245
or within limits, or greater
than/less than checks.

00:12:19.245 --> 00:12:21.370
Those are checks that the
speculative engine simply

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:24.010
doesn't bother with, because
it's just trying to race ahead

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:28.167
and be useful to fetch as much
memory as it can ahead of time.

00:12:28.167 --> 00:12:30.750
Well, it turns out that that's
actually a pretty useful thing,

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:34.060
then, to then find
interesting code gadgets where

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:36.460
you can induce the speculative
engine to race ahead--

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:38.860
read and touch a
bunch of memory,

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:41.860
and then affect the
subsequent behavior

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:43.120
of the speculative engine.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:45.610
And it's that chaining
of those events that

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:48.470
actually leaks information.

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:49.550
The second variant--

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:50.835
Branch Target Injection.

00:12:50.835 --> 00:12:53.210
This is also another behavior
that the speculative engine

00:12:53.210 --> 00:12:53.709
does.

00:12:53.709 --> 00:12:56.720
When it encounters a
branch, it has to decide--

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:58.620
OK, what's most
likely to happen here?

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:01.190
Am I most likely to
expect the branch

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:03.770
will be taken or not taken?

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:07.850
And it turns out you actually
can either find or introduce

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:13.372
or inject interesting branch
target gadgets into the address

00:13:13.372 --> 00:13:15.830
space of the thing that you
want to try to leak information

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:19.190
out-- say it's the
kernel or a Hypervisor--

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:22.634
and use those to sort of, again,
watch and steer that behavior,

00:13:22.634 --> 00:13:24.050
watch the pattern
of memory that's

00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:26.270
accessed, and then
from your own domain,

00:13:26.270 --> 00:13:28.430
time how your memory
accesses work.

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:31.040
And that actually again ends
up leaking bits of information

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:31.834
at a time.

00:13:31.834 --> 00:13:33.500
Repeat that a few
million billion times,

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:35.760
and you've read out the
memory of the machine.

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:36.770
And the last category--

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:38.360
Meltdown.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:40.130
Given a slightly different name.

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:42.971
Given that its behavior
is a little bit different.

00:13:42.971 --> 00:13:44.720
I mentioned these other
ones are more sort

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:48.530
of a speculative behavior, and
branching, and touching memory.

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:50.660
Meltdown was different
in that it's basically

00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:53.050
just a fundamental
privilege check

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:55.910
that the speculative pipeline
doesn't bother to do until it's

00:13:55.910 --> 00:13:57.530
all done with speculation.

00:13:57.530 --> 00:14:01.211
And this is the user kernel
supervisor page table bit.

00:14:01.211 --> 00:14:03.710
And what this means is actually
the speculative engine, when

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:07.220
you're in a user mode process,
will happily go and read

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:09.740
right on through into kernel
memory in the same address

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:10.340
space--

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:12.256
something that's normally
prohibited and would

00:14:12.256 --> 00:14:13.230
generate a page fault.

00:14:13.230 --> 00:14:15.320
Speculative engine
doesn't check that bit

00:14:15.320 --> 00:14:17.430
until the very, very
end of the speculation.

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:20.510
Again, on some processors.

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:23.169
And again, what that means is
you can effectively then induce

00:14:23.169 --> 00:14:24.710
the speculative
engine to read memory

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:28.010
that your program should never
normally be able to read--

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:30.340
time access patterns
leak information out.

00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:36.010
So with the ideas
here of these issues,

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:37.810
I'm going to take a
step back for a moment

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:40.450
and talk a little bit about
our infrastructure stack

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:41.920
and some of the
security philosophy

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:43.780
that we have at Google.

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:48.100
One of our fundamental guiding
principles here at Google

00:14:48.100 --> 00:14:51.220
is a two security boundary rule.

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:52.960
And what that means
is that we always

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:55.460
want to ensure and
engineer into our systems

00:14:55.460 --> 00:14:59.290
at least two distinct security
boundaries, such that no one

00:14:59.290 --> 00:15:02.800
single flaw can allow
malicious code-- hostile code--

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:05.030
to break out.

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:07.720
And in the case for Google
Compute Engine, which

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:09.830
underpins a lot of our
Cloud infrastructure,

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:13.099
including Kubernetes engine
and many other things,

00:15:13.099 --> 00:15:14.890
those two boundaries
happen to be these two

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:15.931
pieces of software here--

00:15:15.931 --> 00:15:21.347
KVM and VMM-- our
purpose-built VMM.

00:15:21.347 --> 00:15:23.680
And I'll talk a little bit
about what each of those are,

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:25.854
and how they compose
in the stack,

00:15:25.854 --> 00:15:27.520
and what the security
properties of them

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:29.380
are, and then how
and where are we

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:33.220
had to apply these
mitigations on top of them.

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:33.720
All right.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:35.160
So what is KVM?

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:39.590
KVM is the hypervisor that's
part of the Linux Kernel.

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:44.010
This is what the majority of
our structure is based on,

00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:46.030
and it's open source.

00:15:46.030 --> 00:15:48.950
Google has invested quite a
lot into finding and fixing

00:15:48.950 --> 00:15:51.890
vulnerabilities in KVM and
getting those vulnerabilities

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:54.170
patched and pushed upstream.

00:15:54.170 --> 00:15:56.000
We've also invested
a significant amount

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:58.260
in reducing the attack
surface in KVM--

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:02.300
reducing complexity,
taking devices out of KVM

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:06.140
into the host kernel and
pushing them up into user space

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:08.300
where they can be further
and better sandboxed

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:11.630
in that user space VMM.

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:13.880
And so the investment
here is to ensure

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:15.890
this is a primary
security boundary,

00:16:15.890 --> 00:16:19.640
and as hardened and as
minimized as possible.

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:22.910
But yet still,
something like Spectre

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:25.910
means that if there is a
vulnerable branch target

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:28.430
inside of KVM, a virtual
machine could potentially

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:31.140
read the memory of the
entire host machine.

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:34.510
It's a significant concern.

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:36.560
And then up in this
user space portion

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:39.170
is a purpose built
Virtual Machine

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:43.272
Manager that Google built. And
why we went with this approach?

00:16:43.272 --> 00:16:45.230
Well, we wanted something
that was really small

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:46.070
in attack surface.

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:48.710
We wanted something that
was built for security

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:51.830
from the ground up, applying
all of Google's best security

00:16:51.830 --> 00:16:56.280
practices and tooling to it.

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:58.580
And we also wanted to
further be able to sandbox

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:01.640
this thing itself, to make
it an even stronger security

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:03.230
boundary.

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:07.339
There's one of these user space
VMMs for every virtual machine

00:17:07.339 --> 00:17:09.140
that runs on the system.

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:11.910
And each one of these is
itself isolated and sandboxed.

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:13.910
So the idea is, if you
think about this multiple

00:17:13.910 --> 00:17:17.300
boundary-type
philosophy, if a workload

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:19.940
were to break out and
compromise its VMM, that

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:22.880
VMM itself is further
sandboxed and unprivileged,

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:25.790
and wouldn't actually give
them any additional information

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:28.880
without some subsequent second
vulnerability in the host

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:31.200
itself.

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:33.944
But again, something
like, say, Meltdown--

00:17:33.944 --> 00:17:36.110
well, now it would mean if
somebody were in the VMM,

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:38.517
they could read through the
Meltdown vulnerability--

00:17:38.517 --> 00:17:40.850
all of the memory of the
hosts, including all the memory

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:42.780
of the other virtual
machines in the platform.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:43.655
That simply won't do.

00:17:43.655 --> 00:17:46.430
So we have to apply and think
about a series of mitigations

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:48.140
here.

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:50.120
So what were those mitigations?

00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:52.970
The first one was something
that we developed.

00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:55.400
An engineer on the Kernel
team at Google, Paul Turner,

00:17:55.400 --> 00:17:57.065
developed called Retpoline.

00:17:57.065 --> 00:18:00.030
And we'll talk about that
in detail in a minute.

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:02.360
But really, the point I
want to emphasize here

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:05.090
is it was our
familiarity with KVM,

00:18:05.090 --> 00:18:06.920
the fact that we
spent so much time

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:09.350
doing vulnerability
discovery and fixing

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:11.960
and attack surface
reduction in KVM,

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:14.900
that allowed us to know
exactly the right places in KVM

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:17.810
that we needed to patch
and apply this Retpoline

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:21.530
fix to address and mitigate that
Spectre variant to that applied

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:22.630
to the Hypervisor.

00:18:22.630 --> 00:18:26.070
Likewise, mentioning Meltdown,
there's a mitigation there.

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:27.985
We also had to apply KTPI.

00:18:27.985 --> 00:18:29.662
That's Kernel
Page-Table Isolation.

00:18:29.662 --> 00:18:31.120
I'll talk about
that in detail here

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:33.270
in the next couple of slides.

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:34.790
But this required
not only for us

00:18:34.790 --> 00:18:38.639
to update the operating system
on our host machines, Linux,

00:18:38.639 --> 00:18:40.430
but also to coordinate
across the industry,

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:42.800
working with all
operating system vendors

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:48.521
to get all OS images patched
with this type of technology.

00:18:48.521 --> 00:18:50.270
And then also there
was that variant one--

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:51.830
that bounds check
overflow-- that

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:55.066
meant going and essentially
working with the compiler team,

00:18:55.066 --> 00:18:56.690
working with the
operating system team,

00:18:56.690 --> 00:18:58.356
and looking for those
vulnerable gadgets

00:18:58.356 --> 00:19:01.640
to excise them and remove them.

00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:05.780
So to go a little bit deeper
here even more into Retpoline,

00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:08.600
what Retpoline was
was the realization

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:10.329
that, like the
speculative engine that's

00:19:10.329 --> 00:19:11.870
racing ahead and
trying to figure out

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:14.810
where things are going to go and
potentially then it could start

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:17.990
leaking bits based
on branch movement

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:21.260
of the speculative engine,
we actually could trick that.

00:19:21.260 --> 00:19:24.800
We actually could trap it into
essentially an infinite loop

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:27.020
that would grab that
speculative engine

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:29.840
and basically make it realize,
well, there's actually really

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:31.250
no interesting
branch here at all

00:19:31.250 --> 00:19:35.030
for it to speculate past
and essentially trap it in.

00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:37.250
And then when it came time
to actually really execute

00:19:37.250 --> 00:19:39.560
past this branch,
we could self-modify

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:41.570
a little bit of that code
and then open up that.

00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:45.260
And the real execution when the
real instructions were issued

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:48.410
would actually then go
past and go beyond it.

00:19:48.410 --> 00:19:50.000
So it's this captive
behavior that

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:54.704
actually is what makes this
Retpoline invention work.

00:19:54.704 --> 00:19:56.120
The other nice
property about this

00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:59.369
is it has very little
impact on performance.

00:19:59.369 --> 00:20:01.910
We can't just, and we don't want
to just turn off speculation

00:20:01.910 --> 00:20:05.510
across the board, because the
performance of most modern CPUs

00:20:05.510 --> 00:20:07.370
would be impacted dramatically.

00:20:07.370 --> 00:20:09.830
But what we can do is
we can very selectively

00:20:09.830 --> 00:20:13.169
surgically insert this into
those vulnerable branch points

00:20:13.169 --> 00:20:15.710
and ensure that the speculative
engine won't leak information

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:17.850
across those.

00:20:17.850 --> 00:20:20.650
Shifting back over to this
other thing, Kernel Page Table

00:20:20.650 --> 00:20:22.830
Isolation, KPTI.

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:24.540
This was some technology
that was derived

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:28.560
from Kaiser research into--

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:31.380
essentially you can
think of this as--

00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:33.000
what we've been
doing for many years

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:36.630
is having a single page table
for both user space and kernel

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:38.370
addresses.

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:40.710
And what that meant is that
if you are in user space,

00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:42.840
normally if you try to
address the kernel memory--

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:45.030
high memory above
a certain address--

00:20:45.030 --> 00:20:46.530
there would be a page fault.

00:20:46.530 --> 00:20:47.460
Can't do that, right?

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:50.310
Page table entry says, well,
this part of the memory address

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:51.240
is for user space.

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:55.150
This part is for privilege
for supervisor for the kernel.

00:20:55.150 --> 00:20:58.590
And the problem is that Meltdown
just blows right past that.

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:02.146
So what we had to do is go ahead
and take the hit to say, OK,

00:21:02.146 --> 00:21:04.020
well, we've been doing
this single page table

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:07.770
strategy in operating
systems for decades

00:21:07.770 --> 00:21:09.854
for performance reasons.

00:21:09.854 --> 00:21:11.520
Because whenever you
switch page tables,

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:13.520
you have to flush all
the past translations

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:15.646
and start over from scratch.

00:21:15.646 --> 00:21:18.270
And if you're switching back and
forth between user and kernel,

00:21:18.270 --> 00:21:19.380
that's really inefficient.

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:21.840
Really slows things down.

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:24.570
But conveniently
a few years back,

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:28.280
modern CPUs started to introduce
this idea of a tag TLB.

00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:31.860
This is a way to divide
up that translation buffer

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:33.570
so that you can have--

00:21:33.570 --> 00:21:36.000
essentially translations
remain in the buffer

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:37.550
between different page tables.

00:21:37.550 --> 00:21:40.209
Well now, rather than
switching from one page table

00:21:40.209 --> 00:21:42.750
to another page table and having
to flush everything and then

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:44.910
rebuild all those
translations again--

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:46.310
really slow process--

00:21:46.310 --> 00:21:49.350
I actually get to
leave them in the TLB.

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:51.600
And then when I switch address
space to address space,

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:54.060
I just switch back and forth
between those translations.

00:21:54.060 --> 00:21:57.660
They remain resident, and I
don't have to flush them out.

00:21:57.660 --> 00:22:01.230
So now with this in place then,
I can split up my page tables.

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:03.870
And that means now that
user mode can't actually

00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:06.390
meltdown through to any other
addresses other than its own,

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:09.420
because there are no kernel
translations available to it.

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:12.450
It actually has to take
the full transition, which

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:14.550
stops the meltdown problem.

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:17.240
And again, thanks
to this tag TLB,

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:19.300
PCID is one particular
implementation--

00:22:19.300 --> 00:22:21.510
the process or
context ID feature--

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:22.530
really speed things up.

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:26.380
Again, this also ended up having
minimal performance impact.

00:22:26.380 --> 00:22:28.650
So I just talked
about the things

00:22:28.650 --> 00:22:31.740
that we did to
patch on the hosts--

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:34.270
this platform, this
infrastructure.

00:22:34.270 --> 00:22:35.850
It was also important
to make sure

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:37.470
that the operating
systems that run

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:41.130
on top of this infrastructure,
to inside these VMs, themselves

00:22:41.130 --> 00:22:43.640
were also patched.

00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:46.544
And so this was very
much a industry.

00:22:46.544 --> 00:22:48.210
Everyone having to
work together to have

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:50.910
this ready to go in time for
the announcement when it became

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:52.604
public.

00:22:52.604 --> 00:22:54.520
And this was a collaboration--
a collaboration

00:22:54.520 --> 00:22:57.330
that was driven by very
experienced vulnerability

00:22:57.330 --> 00:22:59.611
responders like
Google and connections

00:22:59.611 --> 00:23:01.360
and through with the
rest of the industry.

00:23:01.360 --> 00:23:02.610
So now I've talked about this.

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:05.210
Then how do we actually
get this out in production?

00:23:05.210 --> 00:23:07.260
And in particular, get
this out in production

00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:09.259
before the world knew
about it, without actually

00:23:09.259 --> 00:23:12.240
sort of tipping off
what was going on?

00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:16.080
So this is another technology we
have at Google, underlying the

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:19.950
Google Compute Engine
called Live Migration.

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:24.040
Live Migration technology in
many virtualization platforms,

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:26.970
including KVM and our
purpose-built VMM,

00:23:26.970 --> 00:23:29.160
that allow you to move
the entire contents

00:23:29.160 --> 00:23:33.120
of one virtual machine from one
physical host over to another.

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:35.040
And importantly, do
so without actually

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:36.142
disrupting the workload.

00:23:36.142 --> 00:23:38.100
You don't have to restart
the operating system.

00:23:38.100 --> 00:23:40.410
You don't have to
restart the workload.

00:23:40.410 --> 00:23:46.860
You don't even have to
reconnect existing sessions.

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:48.900
Quick overview of
how this works--

00:23:48.900 --> 00:23:52.140
you take a virtual machine, you
start to copy all of its memory

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:54.525
over to another
physical host machine,

00:23:54.525 --> 00:23:56.400
and essentially you're
trying to reconstitute

00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:57.660
that VM over there.

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:00.900
So eventually over time, you
get all the memory copied over.

00:24:00.900 --> 00:24:02.809
But there's always
constant memory churn,

00:24:02.809 --> 00:24:05.100
because the VMs are left
running the entire time you're

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:06.281
doing this.

00:24:06.281 --> 00:24:08.655
But eventually in time, you
get most of the memory states

00:24:08.655 --> 00:24:11.189
seem to cross, and there's
a short blackout period

00:24:11.189 --> 00:24:13.230
where you have to pause
this virtual machine, get

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:15.570
the last little bit of
memory in CPU state,

00:24:15.570 --> 00:24:19.110
and device state moved over
to the other reconstitution

00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:20.730
version of that virtual machine.

00:24:20.730 --> 00:24:24.420
Restart it over there, resteer
all the network connections,

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:26.280
and then let it go again.

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:29.100
And we're able to do
this in just an order

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:32.700
of milliseconds operation, with
very minimal impact, or really

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:35.680
no noticeable workload
impact at all.

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:37.210
And in fact, this is a process.

00:24:37.210 --> 00:24:41.040
This is a system that we apply
and we do regularly at Google--

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:44.820
to patch the host operating
system, to patch the VMM,

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:48.100
to apply microcode updates
to the host machine.

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:49.860
And so as a process
of doing this,

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:52.590
we're able to essentially
move all the workloads off

00:24:52.590 --> 00:24:55.770
of a physical machine, patch
that host machine for whatever

00:24:55.770 --> 00:24:58.560
needs to be done for servicing
or for security reasons,

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:02.010
restart that, move VMs back,
and essentially just shuffle

00:25:02.010 --> 00:25:04.410
through an entire
zone within GCE

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:06.690
and roll out these
patches-- these updates.

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:08.850
Again, without the
workloads being impacted,

00:25:08.850 --> 00:25:12.600
are customers really even being
aware that this is happening?

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:14.550
Amazingly then
with this in place,

00:25:14.550 --> 00:25:18.060
is we were able to apply these
patches in the host for Spectre

00:25:18.060 --> 00:25:21.840
and Meltdown over the course
of the month of December

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:24.630
without anyone noticing.

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:26.400
And not just for GC--
we actually did this

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:30.150
across the entire Google fleet
to make sure things like Gmail

00:25:30.150 --> 00:25:32.880
and G Suite and Search and
Docs and everything else also

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:34.677
had all of these
patches in place.

00:25:34.677 --> 00:25:36.510
And it's kind of
interesting to think about.

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:39.900
A system that starts and
runs four billion containers

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:43.530
a week was able to be
patched for these issues

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:45.780
without anyone noticing
or realizing it

00:25:45.780 --> 00:25:47.970
was happening over
the course of a month.

00:25:47.970 --> 00:25:50.970
I've talked about the technology
that made this possible,

00:25:50.970 --> 00:25:52.980
but also, it took people--

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:55.560
took people and process as well.

00:25:55.560 --> 00:25:57.480
The way that we often
operate with Google

00:25:57.480 --> 00:26:01.210
is sort of as a volunteer
firefighter type model,

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:03.360
where there's a major issue.

00:26:03.360 --> 00:26:05.130
OK, we have to do
something about this.

00:26:05.130 --> 00:26:08.040
Let's pull together people that
are experts in their field,

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:11.220
whether it be compilers or
virtualization or operating

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:15.200
systems or CPU design, as
well as cross discipline.

00:26:15.200 --> 00:26:18.870
It requires communication
and coordination and response

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:20.370
and marketing, PR--

00:26:20.370 --> 00:26:24.460
everyone has to come together
to make this actually work.

00:26:24.460 --> 00:26:26.620
And this is not the
only time we do this.

00:26:26.620 --> 00:26:27.922
We do this quite regularly.

00:26:27.922 --> 00:26:29.880
I'm talking about one
particular incident here.

00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:32.430
I'll talk about another
in just a minute.

00:26:32.430 --> 00:26:34.290
But essentially, I
would say that it's

00:26:34.290 --> 00:26:37.020
this relationship-- it's these
connections that we have that

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:40.110
really make this process work.

00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:42.450
And the end result
is, as I mentioned,

00:26:42.450 --> 00:26:45.810
we were able to patch Google
for Spectre and Meltdown

00:26:45.810 --> 00:26:48.090
without anyone being
affected, and without anyone

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:50.570
realizing it was happening.

00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:52.590
And this is a proven process--

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:54.840
one that essentially
we've established now

00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:58.770
can work against this new
class of vulnerability--

00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:00.870
Spectre and Meltdown
type things.

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:03.330
And it's one that we've
proven in production.

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:04.830
If you want to read
more information

00:27:04.830 --> 00:27:07.347
about the actual details,
go far more detail--

00:27:07.347 --> 00:27:09.180
I could spend an hour
talking about each one

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:10.685
of these different
variants alone.

00:27:10.685 --> 00:27:13.650
I've got the links here to the
more information about that.

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:15.810
Definitely recommend
reading that.

00:27:15.810 --> 00:27:17.490
And now I'm going to
transition to talk

00:27:17.490 --> 00:27:22.050
about another interesting
incident that happened.

00:27:22.050 --> 00:27:26.069
This was a 2014 App
Engine attempted breakout.

00:27:26.069 --> 00:27:28.110
And we'll talk a little
bit about, again, somehow

00:27:28.110 --> 00:27:31.680
this technology applied and
helped to manage and mitigate

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:32.610
this.

00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:35.447
Also a little bit of what it
looked like from the inside.

00:27:35.447 --> 00:27:37.280
And then how all of our
processes and people

00:27:37.280 --> 00:27:39.220
had then come together.

00:27:39.220 --> 00:27:40.200
So quick background.

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:42.870
Some context here.

00:27:42.870 --> 00:27:46.800
In 2014, a security
researcher, Adam Gadawak,

00:27:46.800 --> 00:27:50.370
expert in finding issues with
the Java virtual machine--

00:27:50.370 --> 00:27:54.060
finding ways for Java code
to break out of its sandbox--

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:58.530
started to actively do
research on Google App Engine.

00:27:58.530 --> 00:27:59.550
What is App Engine?

00:27:59.550 --> 00:28:02.370
App Engine is a managed
application platform

00:28:02.370 --> 00:28:05.730
that allows users to
deploy code to Google.

00:28:05.730 --> 00:28:11.160
Whether it's Php, Python, Go,
Java, we take and host that,

00:28:11.160 --> 00:28:13.650
and you don't have to think
about the actual infrastructure

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:15.520
underneath that.

00:28:15.520 --> 00:28:18.040
Well, that Java part is
interesting to a Java security

00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:21.250
researcher, and he wanted
to understand just how

00:28:21.250 --> 00:28:24.040
strong that Java boundary was.

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:29.240
And in fact, we sandbox,
as I mentioned before,

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:31.862
to make sure that the
attack surface is reduced,

00:28:31.862 --> 00:28:33.820
to make sure that the
layer underneath whatever

00:28:33.820 --> 00:28:36.650
the thing is that we
rely on the sandboxes

00:28:36.650 --> 00:28:38.860
is tightly locked down.

00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:42.040
But again, I mentioned
the two-boundary rule.

00:28:42.040 --> 00:28:46.210
So whereas the Java VM was
a boundary in the system,

00:28:46.210 --> 00:28:48.600
it wasn't the only boundary.

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:51.400
And so walking through the
sequence of what happened

00:28:51.400 --> 00:28:53.770
and what this looked like--

00:28:53.770 --> 00:28:56.170
the researcher poking
around the platform,

00:28:56.170 --> 00:28:58.180
trying to look at how
we were structuring

00:28:58.180 --> 00:29:01.240
how things were done, and
where the boundaries were--

00:29:01.240 --> 00:29:03.670
whether his experience
and knowledge in Java

00:29:03.670 --> 00:29:06.630
would apply here as well.

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:08.590
Started to poke and
started to poke.

00:29:08.590 --> 00:29:11.560
What that looked like from our
point of view is on December 5,

00:29:11.560 --> 00:29:14.660
we saw a lot of interesting
alerts and a lot of things

00:29:14.660 --> 00:29:15.660
started to light up.

00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:18.645
Like, somebody is actually
doing something very active

00:29:18.645 --> 00:29:19.520
against the platform.

00:29:19.520 --> 00:29:24.841
Something somewhere, maybe where
they shouldn't necessarily be.

00:29:24.841 --> 00:29:26.840
We didn't know who it
was, what they were doing,

00:29:26.840 --> 00:29:28.756
or why they were doing
this, but we definitely

00:29:28.756 --> 00:29:31.600
knew something was a
serious, major issue.

00:29:31.600 --> 00:29:33.570
So we froze those instances.

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:36.450
We suspended the account to
look at this, understanding

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:39.840
exactly what was going on.

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:43.200
And so what was going on is
we were depending on the Java

00:29:43.200 --> 00:29:45.210
virtual machine as
a first security

00:29:45.210 --> 00:29:46.830
boundary in App Engine.

00:29:46.830 --> 00:29:48.420
We were relying on
that to constrain

00:29:48.420 --> 00:29:50.711
the behavior of the programs
that were deployed so they

00:29:50.711 --> 00:29:53.760
couldn't do things like
issue arbitrary system calls

00:29:53.760 --> 00:29:56.131
or run arbitrary
binaries on the host.

00:29:56.131 --> 00:29:57.630
And we'd gone through
and done a lot

00:29:57.630 --> 00:29:59.430
of meticulous work
in the Java VM

00:29:59.430 --> 00:30:02.010
itself to reduce
that attack surface

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:03.900
and apply these restrictions.

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:06.660
But it wasn't something that
the Java VM was actually really

00:30:06.660 --> 00:30:08.770
originally ever designed for.

00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:11.100
And so it was a process
that we had to apply and add

00:30:11.100 --> 00:30:12.720
onto these things.

00:30:12.720 --> 00:30:15.960
And the security researcher was
finding weaknesses and flaws

00:30:15.960 --> 00:30:18.810
in that boundary.

00:30:18.810 --> 00:30:21.660
What it highlighted to
us is the challenge--

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:26.160
the issues around relying on the
Java VM as a security boundary.

00:30:26.160 --> 00:30:28.410
It helped to motivate
the development

00:30:28.410 --> 00:30:30.957
of stronger sandboxing
technology at Google.

00:30:30.957 --> 00:30:32.790
But the other thing
that was happening here,

00:30:32.790 --> 00:30:34.956
and this was the interesting
part, is the next day--

00:30:34.956 --> 00:30:36.410
so December 6--

00:30:36.410 --> 00:30:39.030
the security researcher went
onto the full disclosure

00:30:39.030 --> 00:30:41.730
security mailing list
and said, hey everybody,

00:30:41.730 --> 00:30:44.340
I've been doing research
on Google App Engine.

00:30:44.340 --> 00:30:46.632
I think I may have poked
a little bit too far

00:30:46.632 --> 00:30:48.090
and gotten a little
too aggressive,

00:30:48.090 --> 00:30:49.090
because I got shut down.

00:30:49.090 --> 00:30:50.941
I got detected and
shut down right away.

00:30:50.941 --> 00:30:51.940
And that was the moment.

00:30:51.940 --> 00:30:53.970
And that was actually the
point at which we realized, OK.

00:30:53.970 --> 00:30:56.070
Well actually, now we
know who was doing this

00:30:56.070 --> 00:30:57.847
and what their motivation was.

00:30:57.847 --> 00:30:59.430
Maybe they should
come and talk to us.

00:30:59.430 --> 00:31:02.726
Maybe we should work together
on fixing these vulnerabilities.

00:31:02.726 --> 00:31:04.350
Because what they
actually had done is,

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:06.140
they'd broken out
of one sandbox,

00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:09.450
but they had landed it right
back into another sandbox--

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:12.330
and a sandbox that wasn't Java.

00:31:12.330 --> 00:31:14.820
Two distinct, different types
of sandboxes-- different

00:31:14.820 --> 00:31:16.720
technologies,
different skill sets,

00:31:16.720 --> 00:31:18.970
different vulnerabilities
required to get through.

00:31:18.970 --> 00:31:21.210
But it was a place where
we could monitor and see

00:31:21.210 --> 00:31:27.350
very anomalous behavior to
respond and shut that down.

00:31:27.350 --> 00:31:30.020
And that moves into new
technology and new sandboxing

00:31:30.020 --> 00:31:34.480
technology that we developed--
something called gVisor.

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:37.550
gVisor is a sandbox
technology that

00:31:37.550 --> 00:31:41.030
was derived from research
into operating system level

00:31:41.030 --> 00:31:42.119
virtualization.

00:31:42.119 --> 00:31:43.910
When we think of
virtualization, we usually

00:31:43.910 --> 00:31:48.650
think either really low-level,
like machine VMs emulating CPU

00:31:48.650 --> 00:31:52.190
and device behavior, or we think
about these language-level VMs,

00:31:52.190 --> 00:31:55.160
like Java.

00:31:55.160 --> 00:31:57.950
The idea with gVisor
is actually there's

00:31:57.950 --> 00:32:01.010
a maybe even more efficient
way to sandbox and isolate

00:32:01.010 --> 00:32:04.940
applications, by emulating
system-called behavior.

00:32:04.940 --> 00:32:07.520
And so essentially, we're
emulating an operating system--

00:32:07.520 --> 00:32:10.430
not a machine, not a
language, but an OS

00:32:10.430 --> 00:32:11.964
at the system-call layer.

00:32:11.964 --> 00:32:13.880
And in fact, with the
development of this now,

00:32:13.880 --> 00:32:16.370
we're able to run the
majority of untrusted code

00:32:16.370 --> 00:32:18.860
at Google on top of gVisor.

00:32:18.860 --> 00:32:21.200
And it's significantly
more efficient than trying

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:23.090
to fall back to
running everything

00:32:23.090 --> 00:32:25.550
into large virtual machines.

00:32:25.550 --> 00:32:28.531
We open sourced that
earlier this year.

00:32:28.531 --> 00:32:30.530
If you're interested to
learn more about gVisor,

00:32:30.530 --> 00:32:33.240
we have an entire talk on
that coming on Thursday.

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:35.660
I'd definitely recommend
going to take a look at that.

00:32:35.660 --> 00:32:37.610
Or go up onto GitHub,
and you can take a look

00:32:37.610 --> 00:32:39.450
at the source code for it.

00:32:39.450 --> 00:32:42.950
So the end result is with
this series of technology

00:32:42.950 --> 00:32:44.780
and our incident
response process

00:32:44.780 --> 00:32:46.850
and the multi-layers
of sandboxing,

00:32:46.850 --> 00:32:49.710
we're able to detect and
constrain and confine

00:32:49.710 --> 00:32:52.250
an attempted breakout
of App Engine

00:32:52.250 --> 00:32:54.147
by a noted security expert.

00:32:54.147 --> 00:32:56.480
The other thing about this
is, in doing so in such a way

00:32:56.480 --> 00:32:58.730
that they were still confined--
didn't impact anybody.

00:32:58.730 --> 00:33:01.010
Didn't impact other
customers, or other user data.

00:33:01.010 --> 00:33:02.780
The other thing
that this leads into

00:33:02.780 --> 00:33:05.300
is the Vulnerability
rewards program at Google,

00:33:05.300 --> 00:33:07.760
and why this interaction
and outreach with security

00:33:07.760 --> 00:33:09.082
researchers is so important.

00:33:09.082 --> 00:33:11.540
We ended up developing this
relationship with this security

00:33:11.540 --> 00:33:15.800
researcher, and to find and fix
more issues within App Engine

00:33:15.800 --> 00:33:17.985
so that customers and
users could be safe.

00:33:17.985 --> 00:33:20.360
To talk a little bit more
about the Vulnerability rewards

00:33:20.360 --> 00:33:23.246
program, I'll hand
it back over to Andy.

00:33:23.246 --> 00:33:26.530
ANDY CHANG: Thanks, Brandon.

00:33:26.530 --> 00:33:29.950
So as Brandon alluded to, the
Vulnerabilty rewards program

00:33:29.950 --> 00:33:32.429
was something that, once
we had the connection

00:33:32.429 --> 00:33:33.970
with the security
researcher, we were

00:33:33.970 --> 00:33:37.330
able to pull them into that
and engage within that.

00:33:37.330 --> 00:33:39.520
And to step back
a bit, the purpose

00:33:39.520 --> 00:33:41.830
of the Vlunerability
rewards program at Google

00:33:41.830 --> 00:33:44.140
is really around turning
what is traditionally

00:33:44.140 --> 00:33:47.950
an adversarial relationship
between security researchers

00:33:47.950 --> 00:33:50.860
and software developers
and software providers

00:33:50.860 --> 00:33:53.890
into something that's
collaborative and creates value

00:33:53.890 --> 00:33:56.690
and wins for the security
researcher themselves,

00:33:56.690 --> 00:33:59.050
for the software provider,
and really most importantly,

00:33:59.050 --> 00:34:00.400
for the end customer.

00:34:00.400 --> 00:34:03.730
As security researchers
get rewarded,

00:34:03.730 --> 00:34:07.330
both in terms of recognition and
actual financial compensation

00:34:07.330 --> 00:34:09.880
for their energy, for their
ingenuity, for their interest

00:34:09.880 --> 00:34:13.389
in digging into things deeply,
and us as software providers

00:34:13.389 --> 00:34:15.010
get the benefit of
that additional set

00:34:15.010 --> 00:34:18.310
of eyes working actively
to improve and find

00:34:18.310 --> 00:34:19.810
flaws in the software.

00:34:19.810 --> 00:34:21.580
And as I said,
again, end customers

00:34:21.580 --> 00:34:25.129
benefit because the quality
of software goes up.

00:34:25.129 --> 00:34:26.920
The Vulnerability
rewards program at Google

00:34:26.920 --> 00:34:30.460
was started in 2010,
over eight years ago.

00:34:30.460 --> 00:34:34.837
And today we've paid out almost
$12 million in rewards to that.

00:34:34.837 --> 00:34:36.920
So in addition to the
recognition-- and some folks

00:34:36.920 --> 00:34:40.360
do this for recognition,
security researchers

00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:43.090
do this to just improve
underlying software,

00:34:43.090 --> 00:34:44.889
and some do do it
for the benefits

00:34:44.889 --> 00:34:46.480
of the financial reward.

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:48.850
But as we look at it,
it really promotes

00:34:48.850 --> 00:34:50.920
things like ethical hacking.

00:34:50.920 --> 00:34:53.560
And over the time, as
the program has evolved,

00:34:53.560 --> 00:34:55.060
we've expanded the
program to cover

00:34:55.060 --> 00:34:58.870
not just Google and Cloud,
but Android and other services

00:34:58.870 --> 00:34:59.710
at Google.

00:34:59.710 --> 00:35:03.460
Each have their own parts of the
Vulnerability rewards program.

00:35:03.460 --> 00:35:05.680
Also as times evolve,
we've continued

00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:08.710
to increase also the
financial rewards tied

00:35:08.710 --> 00:35:12.310
to particular vulnerabilities
that are found.

00:35:12.310 --> 00:35:14.800
Just recently we
elevated the reward

00:35:14.800 --> 00:35:20.902
found for sandbox escapes
from $20,000 to $31,337.

00:35:20.902 --> 00:35:23.110
And if you want to know
where that number comes from,

00:35:23.110 --> 00:35:26.950
you can talk to me
after this session.

00:35:26.950 --> 00:35:28.910
So once we're able to
bring this together,

00:35:28.910 --> 00:35:30.760
the security researcher,
and therefore

00:35:30.760 --> 00:35:33.670
able to really understand
what was going on

00:35:33.670 --> 00:35:36.370
and resolve the
incidents, both we

00:35:36.370 --> 00:35:39.250
were able to then go mitigate
the issues on App Engine,

00:35:39.250 --> 00:35:41.500
and then proceed
forward and start

00:35:41.500 --> 00:35:43.914
looking for the next incident.

00:35:43.914 --> 00:35:46.330
Now, to talk a little bit about
that-- we've talked about,

00:35:46.330 --> 00:35:49.750
and Brandon's covered a lot
of the technologies, process,

00:35:49.750 --> 00:35:51.850
and people around
Spectre and Meltdown.

00:35:51.850 --> 00:35:55.930
And in both Spectre and
Meltdown, and the 2014 App

00:35:55.930 --> 00:35:58.930
Engine breakout,
the overall process

00:35:58.930 --> 00:36:02.380
of how we do detection response
at Google was the same.

00:36:02.380 --> 00:36:07.480
So as an overview, we
have experts in SOCs

00:36:07.480 --> 00:36:12.940
around the world that follow
the clock on a 24/7 basis.

00:36:12.940 --> 00:36:14.470
From an overall
standpoint, when we

00:36:14.470 --> 00:36:16.900
think about the problem
with detection response,

00:36:16.900 --> 00:36:19.310
we actually break
it into two parts.

00:36:19.310 --> 00:36:22.670
First is the underlying
pre-fail component.

00:36:22.670 --> 00:36:25.240
So how do we
proactively understand

00:36:25.240 --> 00:36:27.760
how to assess our
own attack surface--

00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:30.110
our own potential risks
and vulnerabilities.

00:36:30.110 --> 00:36:31.526
What are the things
that are being

00:36:31.526 --> 00:36:33.830
done to specify using policy--

00:36:33.830 --> 00:36:35.890
the things, the
behavior that should be?

00:36:35.890 --> 00:36:37.570
How do we actually
build protections?

00:36:37.570 --> 00:36:40.240
Like we talked about on
the two-boundary rule--

00:36:40.240 --> 00:36:43.030
the sandboxing that
inherently build

00:36:43.030 --> 00:36:47.270
a structure that's difficult for
the aggressors to get through.

00:36:47.270 --> 00:36:50.020
And in a lot of ways,
the two-boundary rule--

00:36:50.020 --> 00:36:54.470
the defense in-depth-- is trying
to flip the story from a case

00:36:54.470 --> 00:36:57.700
where traditionally,
attackers have the benefit.

00:36:57.700 --> 00:37:00.504
They're hiding within
a number of signals.

00:37:00.504 --> 00:37:02.920
And as a defender, there's a
needle in a haystack problem,

00:37:02.920 --> 00:37:05.740
trying to find them in
the midst of everything.

00:37:05.740 --> 00:37:08.260
And flipping this in a
multi-boundary situation

00:37:08.260 --> 00:37:11.230
to where the attacker
has to be really careful,

00:37:11.230 --> 00:37:12.822
because they have
multiple layers

00:37:12.822 --> 00:37:14.530
they actually have to
get through and try

00:37:14.530 --> 00:37:15.770
to escape detection.

00:37:15.770 --> 00:37:18.790
And that's trying to balance
out that attacker/defender

00:37:18.790 --> 00:37:20.270
relationship.

00:37:20.270 --> 00:37:22.990
In addition to the pre-fail
pieces and the protections,

00:37:22.990 --> 00:37:25.120
we then obviously have
monitoring logging,

00:37:25.120 --> 00:37:28.390
which allow us to both
understand if the pre-fail

00:37:28.390 --> 00:37:31.900
configurations and
specifications we've let drift,

00:37:31.900 --> 00:37:34.530
as well as to handle
the post-fail case--

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:37.340
to actually detect when
there is an actual incident.

00:37:37.340 --> 00:37:40.720
And as Brandon outlined
before in the two examples,

00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:42.490
go through the
rest of the process

00:37:42.490 --> 00:37:45.010
to bring in that volunteer
firefighter approach-- bring

00:37:45.010 --> 00:37:48.460
in experts around the
different aspects of Google

00:37:48.460 --> 00:37:49.840
to address these things.

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:52.900
And when we think about the
post-fail detection response

00:37:52.900 --> 00:37:55.150
process, it is also multistaged.

00:37:55.150 --> 00:37:58.060
There's the process of detection
itself in the signals database

00:37:58.060 --> 00:38:00.270
that we've built out
to be able to do this,

00:38:00.270 --> 00:38:02.980
the alerting, the actual
response itself, which

00:38:02.980 --> 00:38:06.910
can be either straightforward
or significantly complex

00:38:06.910 --> 00:38:09.460
when something's at the scale
of Spectre and Meltdown.

00:38:09.460 --> 00:38:10.720
And then communications.

00:38:10.720 --> 00:38:13.390
Communications internally,
communications with the product

00:38:13.390 --> 00:38:15.880
teams, communications to
customers, communications

00:38:15.880 --> 00:38:19.210
to other entities that are
involved in the situation.

00:38:19.210 --> 00:38:21.760
And then ultimately for
us, the learning feedback.

00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:24.550
So both at a human
level, a process level,

00:38:24.550 --> 00:38:26.050
and also machine
learning technology

00:38:26.050 --> 00:38:27.830
level so that we're better.

00:38:27.830 --> 00:38:29.620
And so that each
step, each next thing,

00:38:29.620 --> 00:38:33.040
we can further reduce
the time to detection

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:35.600
and further reduce the
time to mitigation.

00:38:35.600 --> 00:38:38.020
So our security teams
have the benefit

00:38:38.020 --> 00:38:40.970
of very integrated
forensics set of tools,

00:38:40.970 --> 00:38:45.100
whether it's to do live memory
forensics or disc forensics,

00:38:45.100 --> 00:38:48.190
timeline analysis, and tie
all these different threads

00:38:48.190 --> 00:38:49.060
together.

00:38:49.060 --> 00:38:52.390
And also the automation
to do that at scale.

00:38:52.390 --> 00:38:54.940
We've actually open-sourced
a number of these tools

00:38:54.940 --> 00:38:57.550
so that our customers and
the community in general

00:38:57.550 --> 00:38:58.210
can benefit.

00:38:58.210 --> 00:39:00.040
The folks of you who
are in the industry

00:39:00.040 --> 00:39:03.447
are probably familiar with
Grr, Turbinia, Recall--

00:39:03.447 --> 00:39:05.530
these are a number of the
tools we use every day--

00:39:05.530 --> 00:39:06.550
Timesketch.

00:39:06.550 --> 00:39:09.730
These are all now available
on Git as open source tooling

00:39:09.730 --> 00:39:12.460
that you can use yourself
to also build out

00:39:12.460 --> 00:39:15.130
your forensics toolkit.

00:39:15.130 --> 00:39:18.610
And one of the other
things is, as probably you

00:39:18.610 --> 00:39:22.420
might anticipate, Google is
one of the most targeted sites

00:39:22.420 --> 00:39:23.780
across the world.

00:39:23.780 --> 00:39:26.290
So from that benefit,
our security teams

00:39:26.290 --> 00:39:27.910
have to be ever vigilant.

00:39:27.910 --> 00:39:30.730
And there's the classic
problem of garrison fatigue.

00:39:30.730 --> 00:39:32.800
If you're vigilant
on standing on guard

00:39:32.800 --> 00:39:35.410
for an extended
period of time and you

00:39:35.410 --> 00:39:38.370
don't get many actual
incidents or pieces

00:39:38.370 --> 00:39:40.120
with these systems
that are protecting us,

00:39:40.120 --> 00:39:42.370
how do you still
retain that vigilance?

00:39:42.370 --> 00:39:45.550
So we have an active culture
within Google of red teaming--

00:39:45.550 --> 00:39:47.080
of Google on Google.

00:39:47.080 --> 00:39:48.640
And that's the key thing.

00:39:48.640 --> 00:39:50.500
And again, uses
the same philosophy

00:39:50.500 --> 00:39:52.460
we use in our instant response.

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:54.640
It's a volunteer
firefighter approach.

00:39:54.640 --> 00:39:56.830
So we get some of
the key experts

00:39:56.830 --> 00:39:59.950
to go hack sometimes and
attack their own code--

00:39:59.950 --> 00:40:02.140
other folks' code
within the products.

00:40:02.140 --> 00:40:04.810
And that kind of both energy
and spirit, as well as

00:40:04.810 --> 00:40:08.170
that really putting the highest
level expertise and visibility

00:40:08.170 --> 00:40:10.360
on particular
types of red teams,

00:40:10.360 --> 00:40:14.860
enables us ourselves to quickly
find vulnerabilities and flaws

00:40:14.860 --> 00:40:15.760
in the system.

00:40:15.760 --> 00:40:17.200
Though we don't
pay out of the VRP

00:40:17.200 --> 00:40:22.580
for our own internal employees,
to much to their unhappiness.

00:40:22.580 --> 00:40:25.780
So if you look at the overall
threat detection pipeline

00:40:25.780 --> 00:40:29.530
once we see something,
you see that cycle

00:40:29.530 --> 00:40:32.270
of looking at logs-- observing.

00:40:32.270 --> 00:40:33.916
Then actually
orientating in terms

00:40:33.916 --> 00:40:35.290
of figuring out
from the security

00:40:35.290 --> 00:40:37.060
analytics what's going on.

00:40:37.060 --> 00:40:41.440
Actually making decisions about
from the underlying pieces,

00:40:41.440 --> 00:40:42.370
what to do.

00:40:42.370 --> 00:40:44.620
And ultimately taking
those remediations.

00:40:44.620 --> 00:40:48.200
The top part of that is
technology and process centric.

00:40:48.200 --> 00:40:51.250
So we have the advantage of
large scale data ingestion

00:40:51.250 --> 00:40:53.284
pipelines, threat direct
detection pipelines.

00:40:53.284 --> 00:40:54.700
Some of those
things you guys have

00:40:54.700 --> 00:40:56.890
seen as a part of
Google Cloud platform.

00:40:56.890 --> 00:40:59.560
BigQuery is something
that we use internally

00:40:59.560 --> 00:41:03.190
as well to do a lot of our
signals and logs processing.

00:41:03.190 --> 00:41:05.580
And then from the
ingestion standpoint,

00:41:05.580 --> 00:41:09.490
the ability to really take in
and handle all that scale data.

00:41:09.490 --> 00:41:12.550
The last point I want to
reemphasize what Brandon talked

00:41:12.550 --> 00:41:15.190
about as well, is you could
have the greatest technology,

00:41:15.190 --> 00:41:16.960
you can have the
greatest process--

00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:20.719
you also need really, really
strong people in this space.

00:41:20.719 --> 00:41:22.510
We are very fortunate
to have the advantage

00:41:22.510 --> 00:41:24.850
of a lot of experienced
folks in this space.

00:41:24.850 --> 00:41:27.560
And as I think you've
heard throughout this talk,

00:41:27.560 --> 00:41:29.860
given all the different
aspects of some of these

00:41:29.860 --> 00:41:31.943
vulnerabilities, especially
something like Spectre

00:41:31.943 --> 00:41:34.930
and Meltdown, the
multi-disciplinary nature

00:41:34.930 --> 00:41:36.640
of the skillset needed--

00:41:36.640 --> 00:41:39.640
readily available in-house
at Google to address things,

00:41:39.640 --> 00:41:42.760
whether its processor
architecture, compilers, OS,

00:41:42.760 --> 00:41:46.810
virtualization,
underlying hardware--

00:41:46.810 --> 00:41:49.030
all those pieces
can come together.

00:41:49.030 --> 00:41:50.890
And then, an equally
important thing

00:41:50.890 --> 00:41:54.190
is, as one of the
largest targets, also

00:41:54.190 --> 00:41:57.850
one of the biggest contributors
to security for the community,

00:41:57.850 --> 00:42:02.470
we have tier one relationships
with other similarly skilled

00:42:02.470 --> 00:42:04.240
organizations and entities.

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:06.400
And that allows us
to be in the loop

00:42:06.400 --> 00:42:08.540
for a lot of these discussions.

00:42:08.540 --> 00:42:11.610
So as I mentioned previously,
the SOC that we use

00:42:11.610 --> 00:42:16.270
is 24/7 around the world, and
the underlying teams really

00:42:16.270 --> 00:42:18.520
include not only the
subject matter experts,

00:42:18.520 --> 00:42:21.670
but the mix of security
engineers, analysts, and also

00:42:21.670 --> 00:42:22.960
forensics experts.

00:42:22.960 --> 00:42:24.460
And so the bottom
half of this loop,

00:42:24.460 --> 00:42:27.520
then, is really around
the human aspect of it.

00:42:27.520 --> 00:42:29.930
Top half being
technology and process.

00:42:29.930 --> 00:42:32.440
We also need the humans to
actually make the decisions--

00:42:32.440 --> 00:42:34.510
the right decisions, as
Brandon talked about.

00:42:34.510 --> 00:42:36.970
Where to put mitigations
like Retpoline,

00:42:36.970 --> 00:42:39.910
and where to actually do
those with lowest impact

00:42:39.910 --> 00:42:42.890
on overall performance.

00:42:42.890 --> 00:42:48.220
So to summarize, given something
at the scale of Spectre

00:42:48.220 --> 00:42:51.900
and Meltdown, which again,
were fundamental flaws in most

00:42:51.900 --> 00:42:55.570
modern microprocessors that
impacted the entire industry,

00:42:55.570 --> 00:43:00.670
for someone who is a Google
customer, it was a nonevent.

00:43:00.670 --> 00:43:02.500
Customers didn't
have to do anything.

00:43:02.500 --> 00:43:04.900
Customers were not impacted.

00:43:04.900 --> 00:43:09.040
And that really is the nature of
some of the security and design

00:43:09.040 --> 00:43:10.840
infrastructure that
we've put in place,

00:43:10.840 --> 00:43:15.450
and the ongoing aspiration
of how the team works.

00:43:15.450 --> 00:43:17.322
And then to summarize
then as we've

00:43:17.322 --> 00:43:18.780
talked about through
this, in order

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:20.980
to achieve that type
of result, we really

00:43:20.980 --> 00:43:24.760
do need proven technology,
process, and people.

00:43:24.760 --> 00:43:28.000
And it starts with the core
philosophy around Google

00:43:28.000 --> 00:43:31.750
that Brandon and I both talked
about-- this defense in depth.

00:43:31.750 --> 00:43:33.670
No single point of failure.

00:43:33.670 --> 00:43:37.300
That at least two-boundary rule
built across all the services

00:43:37.300 --> 00:43:39.580
and all the components
within Google,

00:43:39.580 --> 00:43:42.100
where we are very fortunate
to have that ability

00:43:42.100 --> 00:43:44.810
to have an end-to-end
ownership of the hardware

00:43:44.810 --> 00:43:47.560
and software, many a
things purpose-built,

00:43:47.560 --> 00:43:49.810
so then also that deep
understanding of the underlying

00:43:49.810 --> 00:43:50.790
technology.

00:43:50.790 --> 00:43:54.010
So to know where to put
mitigations and fixes where

00:43:54.010 --> 00:43:57.970
they have the highest impact on
the actual security instance,

00:43:57.970 --> 00:44:01.870
and the lowest impact on
end customer performance.

00:44:01.870 --> 00:44:05.590
As I think Diane highlighted
in the keynote, the consistency

00:44:05.590 --> 00:44:07.960
of our infrastructure--
homogeneity,

00:44:07.960 --> 00:44:11.950
the kind of ability for us
to manage our infrastructure

00:44:11.950 --> 00:44:15.010
in the commonly-used phrase,
"as cattles, not pets,"

00:44:15.010 --> 00:44:17.920
so that you can have
automation, scalability,

00:44:17.920 --> 00:44:19.820
and you can do
patching, detection--

00:44:19.820 --> 00:44:21.790
all of these at scale.

00:44:21.790 --> 00:44:24.430
And then really, the proven
response and remediation

00:44:24.430 --> 00:44:25.360
process--

00:44:25.360 --> 00:44:27.200
at the end of the
day, that experienced

00:44:27.200 --> 00:44:29.140
multi-disciplinary team.

00:44:29.140 --> 00:44:31.030
So we designed
the solutions that

00:44:31.030 --> 00:44:34.090
addressed the flaws found
in Spectre and Meltdown

00:44:34.090 --> 00:44:36.490
so that they could be
mitigated across all of Google

00:44:36.490 --> 00:44:37.930
and for all Google's customers.

00:44:37.930 --> 00:44:41.450
And we applied patches to
the full fleet of Google,

00:44:41.450 --> 00:44:44.290
including GC, in
one single month,

00:44:44.290 --> 00:44:46.690
before these
attacks were public.

00:44:46.690 --> 00:44:49.600
With again, no
effort from customers

00:44:49.600 --> 00:44:52.029
and no noticeable
impact on them.

00:44:52.029 --> 00:44:53.820
So with that, I'd like
to conclude the talk

00:44:53.820 --> 00:44:57.040
and invite Brandon back up so
we can take some questions.

00:44:57.040 --> 00:44:59.200
I think there is a
floor mic at the end

00:44:59.200 --> 00:45:00.790
if you guys have any questions.

00:45:00.790 --> 00:45:03.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

